  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 114 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nThrough Sunday Night:\n\nOngoing rain showers are being observed south of the Kankakee \nRiver Valley this afternoon ahead of a mid level wave that is \npassing over the area. There has been an increase in shower \ncoverage over the area, being located under the right entrance \nregion of a 300 mb jet. However, lapse rates are fairly poor \nwith the better well of instability south of the forecast area. \nA rogue lightning strike or two may occur, but confidence is \nlow. This mid level wave should continue to pass south and east \nover the forecast area through the afternoon with chances for \nshowers diminishing toward sunset.\n\nCloud cover over the area today has help mute temperatures \nslightly. Clearing behind the wave should allow for temperatures\nnorthwest of the Fox Valley to increase quickly, but opted to \nlower this afternoon's high temperatures else by a degree or \ntwo. As the wave exits to the east, clearer skies can be \nexpected through the overnight north of I-80 (with lingering \ncloud cover to the south). Winds are expected to become light \nand variable overnight. With better radiational cooling due to \nthe lack of cloud cover, there is a chance for another round of \nfog to develop toward daybreak tomorrow morning. Better chances \nremain north and west of Chicago's urban core, but satellite and\nobservations will need to be monitored through the night for \nany fog development.\n\nSurface high pressure will build tomorrow under the influence \nof a weak ridge passing overhead. Sunny conditions are expected\nwith light winds to provide quite an ideal weather day \ntomorrow. As the ridge passes east overnight into Saturday \nmorning, models are projecting a very weak shortwave to pass \nover Wisconsin. There is a non-zero chance that there could be \nsome light showers/sprinkles on Saturday morning as the wave \npasses over, but models are a.) keeping the track of the wave a \nlittle farther north in Wisconsin and into western Michigan and \nb.) struggling to penetrate a drier air mass over Illinois. \nModels are suggesting that the surface cold front associated \nwith the wave could potentially slow and wash out over I- 80 by \nSunday morning. Perhaps some light showers develop along the \nfront on Sunday morning, but again, the drier air and weak \nforcing should limit coverage, assuming it happens at all. \nOtherwise, this weekend is looking fairly pleasant. Southerly \nwinds on Saturday will help drive temperatures back up into the \n80s. There is a little more uncertainty with temperatures on \nSunday due to the timing of the frontal passage. An early \narrival will turn winds to the northwest and usher in cooler, \nmore seasonable temps in the 70s. But areas ahead of the front \nsouth of I-80 have another chance of warming back up into the \n80s.\n\nDK\n\nMonday through Thursday:\n\nThe first half of next week continues to look like it will turn\nmore active as we get into more of a southwesterly flow aloft. \nThere will be multiple opportunities for showers and \nthunderstorms Monday-Wednesday as several shortwave troughs \nripple through the southwesterly flow. It remains too early to \npinpoint timing or rainfall amounts, but there is better chances\nfor a transition to a generally wetter, more unsettled pattern \nnext week.\n\nIzzi/DK",
